  "answer": "We should connect electric appliances in parallel in a household circuit because, in a parallel connection, each appliance receives the same potential difference (voltage) from the power supply. This ensures that each appliance operates at its rated voltage, allowing it to function properly. Additionally, if one appliance fails or is turned off, the others continue to work, as the circuit is not interrupted. In contrast, if appliances are connected in series, the current through all appliances is the same, and the total resistance increases. This can lead to a significant drop in current, causing the appliances to operate inefficiently or not at all. Moreover, if one appliance in a series circuit breaks down, the entire circuit is disrupted, and none of the appliances will work. This is why household circuits are designed with appliances in parallel.",
